narrative:
## Building a Multi-Module Program

A well-organized program has modules for distinct responsibilities. Each module handles one concern.

### Four Modules, One Program

```xiom
module models {
  pub type User = {
    name: Str;
    email: Str;
  }

  pub fn new_user(name: Str, email: Str) -> User {
    return User{ name: name, email: email };
  }
}

module validation {
  pub fn is_valid_email(email: Str) -> Bool {
    return email.contains("@") & email.contains(".");
  }
}

module display {
  pub fn show_user(user: &models.User) {
    io.println(user.name + " <" + user.email + ">");
  }
}

module app {
  pub fn register_user(name: Str, email: Str) -> Str {
    if validation.is_valid_email(email) {
      let user = models.new_user(name, email);
      display.show_user(&user);
      return "Registered!";
    }
    return "Invalid email";
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let result = app.register_user("Alex", "alex@site.com");
  io.println(result);

  let bad_result = app.register_user("Bob", "bad_email");
  io.println(bad_result);
  return 0;
}
```

### Module Dependency Diagram

```
main
  -> app (orchestrator)
       -> models (data types)
       -> validation (business rules)
       -> display (output formatting)
```

### Separation of Concerns

| Module | Responsibility | Knows About |
|--------|---------------|-------------|
| models | Data types | Nothing (leaf module) |
| validation | Rules checking | Nothing (leaf module) |
| display | Output formatting | models (for types) |
| app | Orchestration | models, validation, display |

### Why This Structure Wins

- **models** can change fields without affecting validation logic
- **validation** can add new rules without touching display code
- **display** can change formatting without affecting business logic
- **app** just wires them together — it's thin, readable, and testable

This is the foundation of **layered architecture**: each layer depends on the layers below it, never above.

